            <ArticleTitle>Embolic myocardial infarction and left ventricular rupture due to mitral valve endocarditis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>206-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A left ventricular rupture due to embolic myocardial infarction is extremely rare. A 72-year-old woman developed an acute embolic myocardial infarction and mitral regurgitation due to infective endocarditis. Two days after the infarction, a left ventricular free wall rupture occurred after transesophageal echo examination. She received an epicardial patch and mitral valve replacement. Perioperatively, an intra-aortic balloon pump and long-term antibiotics were used. The postoperative course was uneventful, and she is doing well 10 months after surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
